⭐ L5 Interview Answer

I'd refine your answer to this:

"Since the requirement is to return all subsets, 
 I can model the problem as a binary decision tree. 

 For every element, I have exactly two choices: include it in the current subset or exclude it.
 My recursive state consists of the current index and the current path. 

 When the index reaches the end of the array, I have made a decision for every element, 
 so I copy the path into the result. 

 I use backtracking to restore the path after exploring the include branch 
 so that I can explore the exclude branch from the same parent state.

 There are 2^n possible subsets, and copying each subset can take O(n), 
  so the total time complexity is O(n·2^n), excluding only constant-time bookkeeping."

That's the structure I want you to develop.



7. Complexity

           There are:  2^n  subsets.
           
           Each subset can contain up to n elements, and we have to copy it into the result.
           
           Therefore:
           
                Time: O(n · 2^n)
                Auxiliary space:  O(n)           
                                  for:                           
                                      recursion stack
